+  Game outputs
+  ============
+  Every title publishes its lamp latch as cabinet outputs (see osd/outputs.h).
+  `<outputs>` adds state the cabinet never latched but a peripheral wants: a
+  Model 2 gun cabinet has no recoil, so nothing on the board says that a shot
+  was fired. The program knows, and these read it from its work RAM
+  (0x00500000-0x005fffff), once a frame.
+
+  `<lamp>` names one bit of the lamp latch. `<value>` is a number at `address`;
+  `type` is "u8" (the default), "u16", "s16", "u32" or "float", little-endian,
+  and anything below zero is published as zero. Its `<when>` children are
+  conditions that must all hold -- the byte at `address`, masked with `mask`
+  (default 0xff), equal to `equals`, or with no `equals` anything but zero --
+  for variables that only mean something while a player is in the game. While
+  one fails the value is zero.
+
+  `<flag>` is 1 while the `<value>` named by `source` is above zero. `<pulse>`
+  is 1 for `frames` frames (default 3) each time that value falls, or with
+  edge="rise" rises, from one frame to the next while its conditions hold. A
+  source has to be declared before what follows it.
+
+  The names are the ones DemulShooter established for these games (P1_Ammo,
+  P1_Clip, P1_CtmRecoil, P1_Life, P1_Damaged, P1_LmpStart, P1_GunMotor,
+  Credits), which is what the game files of MAMEHooker and Hook Of The Reaper
+  listen for. The fighting games add P1_Wins and P2_Wins, the rounds each side
+  has taken in the current match, for a scoreboard. The addresses belong to one
+  revision of a program, so a clone does not inherit its parent's `<outputs>`.
+
   `gearbox="true"` marks a title whose IN1 bits 0x70 read a gear selector
   rather than buttons (MAME's daytona_gearbox_r). `drive_board="true"` marks
   one that latches force-feedback commands on port E. `drive_protocol` says how
